You are debugging a production issue where a financial service fails to maintain transactional integrity. A payment processor debits an account and asynchronously triggers an auditing/fraud service. When fraud is detected and an exception is thrown in the asynchronous block, the audit fails, but the debit transaction committed successfully anyway, causing funds to disappear without an audit trail.

java
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    @Autowired
    private AccountRepository accountRepo;
    @Autowired
    private AuditRepository auditRepo;

    @Transactional
    public CompletableFuture<Void> processPayment(Long accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        accountRepo.debit(accountId, amount);
        return CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
            auditRepo.recordAudit(accountId, amount);
            if (amount.compareTo(new BigDecimal("10000")) > 0) {
                throw new HighValueFraudException("Fraud detected");
            }
        });
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput microservice migrated to Java 21 Virtual Threads (`Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()`). Instead of observing improved concurrency under heavy I/O load, response times degraded exponentially, carrier thread starvation occurred, and CPU utilization became erratic. Java Flight Recorder (JFR) profiling highlights numerous `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` events.

java
public class RemoteConfigurationService {
    private final Map<String, String> configCache = new HashMap<>();
    private final HttpClient httpClient = HttpClient.newHttpClient();

    public synchronized String getConfiguration(String key) {
        if (!configCache.containsKey(key)) {
            HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                    .uri(URI.create("https://config.internal.net/keys/" + key))
                    .build();
            try {
                // Blocking network call inside synchronized monitor
                HttpResponse<String> response = httpClient.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());
                configCache.put(key, response.body());
            } catch (Exception e) {
                throw new RuntimeException(e);
            }
        }
        return configCache.get(key);
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where an API Gateway fans out calls to 10 downstream services in parallel using `CompletableFuture.allOf()`. If one critical downstream dependency immediately fails with an `HttpTimeoutException` or `ConnectException`, the orchestrating service does not fail-fast. Instead, the gateway continues waiting for all remaining downstream calls to finish their long execution cycles before throwing the exception, leading to massive p99 latency spikes.

java
public List<String> fetchAggregatedData(List<Supplier<String>> downstreamCalls) {
    List<CompletableFuture<String>> futures = downstreamCalls.stream()
            .map(call -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(call, customExecutor))
            .toList();

    CompletableFuture<Void> allOf = CompletableFuture.allOf(
            futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0])
    );

    // This line blocks until ALL futures complete, even if one failed immediately
    allOf.join();

    return futures.stream()
            .map(CompletableFuture::join)
            .toList();
}
